WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.827
When a scalar query feels confusing, isolate the inner query mentally.

00:00:03.827 --> 00:00:07.257
Ask what it returns. Here it returns one average age.

00:00:07.257 --> 00:00:10.617
Now imagine replacing the parenthesized SELECT with that number.

00:00:10.617 --> 00:00:13.642
The outer query becomes an ordinary age comparison.

00:00:13.642 --> 00:00:17.952
This simple mental substitution works well for many scalar subqueries.

00:00:17.952 --> 00:00:22.532
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:22.532 --> 00:00:28.032
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:28.032 --> 00:00:30.227
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:30.227 --> 00:00:35.437
In a real setting such as a hospital identifying patients above a benchmark or with no follow-up,

00:00:35.437 --> 00:00:40.892
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:40.892 --> 00:00:42.387
before discussing syntax.

00:00:42.387 --> 00:00:47.760
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
